NAME
       bzip2 - compress or expand files with bzip2 (also bunzip2, bzcat)

SYNOPSIS
       bzip2 [-d] [-c] [-k] [-f] [-v] [-t] [-1...-9] [FILE...]
       bunzip2 [-c] [-k] FILE.bz2...
       bzcat FILE.bz2...

DESCRIPTION
       bzip2 compresses each FILE and replaces it with FILE.bz2, keeping the
       owner, permissions and times. bunzip2 (the same as bzip2 -d) turns a
       .bz2 file back into the original and removes the .bz2. bzcat writes
       the expanded contents to standard output and leaves the file alone.

       bzip2 usually makes smaller files than gzip but is slower. It packs
       one file at a time: to compress a whole directory, make a tar archive
       (tar -cjf archive.tar.bz2 DIR does both steps).

       With no FILE, it reads standard input and writes standard output.

OPTIONS
       -d, --decompress
              Expand instead of compress.

       -c, --stdout
              Write to standard output and keep the input file.

       -k, --keep
              Keep the input file.

       -f, --force
              Overwrite an existing output file.

       -t, --test
              Check that a compressed file is intact.

       -v, --verbose
              Show the compression ratio of each file.

       -1 ... -9
              Block size from 100k to 900k; -9 (the default) compresses best.

EXAMPLES
       Compress a log, keeping the original:

           bzip2 -k /root/messages.log

       Expand it again:

           bunzip2 /root/messages.log.bz2

       Make and unpack a compressed archive of /etc:

           tar -cjf /root/etc.tar.bz2 /etc
           tar -xjf /root/etc.tar.bz2 -C /tmp

SEE ALSO
       gzip(1), xz(1), tar(1)
